      "id": 1,
      "text": "Pisgah National Forest, established in 1915, is North Carolina’s oldest national forest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://northcarolinahistory.org/encyclopedia/pisgah-national-forest/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The forest contains the first tract of land bought under the 1911 Weeks Act, sparking eastern U.S. national forest creation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.fs.usda.gov/r08/northcarolina/recreation/pisgah-national-forest",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pisgah’s name comes from Reverend James Hall, who called the mountains Mount Pisgah during a 1776 Cherokee campaign.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://northcarolinahistory.org/encyclopedia/pisgah-national-forest/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area hosts the Cradle of Forestry in America, preserving the first U.S. forestry school founded in 1898.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.fs.usda.gov/r08/northcarolina/recreation/pisgah-national-forest",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Over 500,000 acres of Pisgah are hardwood forest, whitewater rivers, and hundreds of miles of trails.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.fs.usda.gov/r08/northcarolina/recreation/pisgah-national-forest",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
